---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [["How do I check my DKIM key length?", "Use our DKIM Record Checker: it estimates the key size from the published public key material and flags keys under 2048 bits."], ["Can I use ed25519 instead of RSA for DKIM?", "Yes, RFC 8463 defines Ed25519 for DKIM. It produces shorter keys and is faster, but not all receivers support it yet. RSA 2048 is the safest choice."]];

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: "DKIM Key Length",
  description: "DKIM key length explained: 1024-bit vs 2048-bit RSA keys, why 1024 is weak, and how to check your key size.",
  inLanguage: 'en',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PromptDNS', url: 'https://promptdns.com/' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PromptDNS', url: 'https://promptdns.com/', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://promptdns.com/favicon.svg' } },
  datePublished: '2026-10-27',
  dateModified: '2026-10-27',
};

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "How do I check my DKIM key length?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Use our DKIM Record Checker: it estimates the key size from the published public key material and flags keys under 2048 bits." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can I use ed25519 instead of RSA for DKIM?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Yes, RFC 8463 defines Ed25519 for DKIM. It produces shorter keys and is faster, but not all receivers support it yet. RSA 2048 is the safest choice." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "How do I rotate from 1024-bit to 2048-bit safely?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Publish the new 2048-bit key under a second selector, switch signing to it, verify inbound checks pass, then remove the old selector. Never delete the old record first." } }
  ],
};

const breadcrumbs = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://promptdns.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Email Authentication', item: 'https://promptdns.com/email-authentication/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: "DKIM Key Length" },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DKIM Key Length: 1024 vs 2048-bit RSA Keys and Why 1024 Is Weak"
  description="DKIM key length explained: 1024-bit vs 2048-bit RSA keys, why 1024 is weak, and how to check your key size."
  schema={[article, faqSchema, breadcrumbs]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DKIM Key Length</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DKIM Key Length</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-dim">DKIM key length explained: 1024 vs 2048-bit RSA keys, why 1024 is considered weak, and how to check your key size.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="not-prose my-8" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class='pdns-table' style='width:100%'><thead><tr><th class='whitespace-nowrap'></th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>1024-bit RSA</th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>2048-bit RSA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Security</td><td>Weak: factorable with modern hardware</td><td>Strong: no known practical attacks</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Receiver acceptance</td><td>Rejected by Gmail and some providers</td><td>Accepted by all major providers</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>DNS record size</td><td>~180 characters (fits in one TXT string)</td><td>~380 characters (may need two TXT strings)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>Recommendation</td><td>Upgrade immediately</td><td>Current standard</td></tr></tbody></table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="rich-text mt-10 max-w-none">
      <h2>Technical details</h2>
      <p class='mt-3'>A DKIM key length of 1024 bits is considered weak because modern factoring algorithms can potentially break RSA keys of this size, exposing the private signing key.</p><p class='mt-3'>RFC 6376 does not mandate a minimum key size, but major mailbox providers including Gmail enforce a practical minimum of 1024 bits and recommend 2048 bits for new deployments.</p><p class='mt-3'>A 2048-bit RSA key produces a public key of approximately 380 base64-encoded characters, which may require splitting across two DNS TXT strings due to the 255-character per-string limit.</p>

      <aside class="mt-8 rounded-xl border-l-3 border-l-violet/50 bg-violet/5 p-5" aria-label="Practitioner field notes">
        <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-violet mb-2">Field notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim italic">I have seen 1024-bit keys in production on enterprise domains that were set up years ago and never rotated. The fix is straightforward: generate a new 2048-bit key in your email provider's settings, publish it under a new selector, verify it, and then delete the old record.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I check my DKIM key length?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Use our DKIM Record Checker: it estimates the key size from the published public key material and flags keys under 2048 bits.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I use ed25519 instead of RSA for DKIM?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, RFC 8463 defines Ed25519 for DKIM. It produces shorter keys and is faster, but not all receivers support it yet. RSA 2048 is the safest choice.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I rotate from 1024-bit to 2048-bit safely?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Publish the new 2048-bit key under a second selector, switch signing to it, verify inbound checks pass, then remove the old selector. Never delete the old record first.</p></details>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Check your domain with our free tools:
        <a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF checker</a>,
        <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC checker</a>,
        <a href="/dkim-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DKIM checker</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
